course: dictionary
lesson: 7549
title: Prefixes: the start of a word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A prefix is a group of letters added to the start of a word. It changes the meaning of the word, but the main part stays the same. «un» + «happy» makes «unhappy», which means not happy. Once you know a prefix, you can guess the meaning of many new words.
explain: The main part of a word is called the root. A prefix goes in front of the root, and a suffix goes at the end. Look at «redo»: re- means again, so redo means do again. To spell a new word, write the prefix, then the whole word, letter for letter, so you do not drop any letters.
explain: Prefixes also show size in maths and science. Kilo- means a thousand, so a kilometre is a thousand metres. Centi- means a hundredth, and milli- means a thousandth. To prefix something is to add a word, letters or numbers in front of it.
fact: A prefix is a group of letters added to the start of a word to make a new word with a different meaning.
fact: «Un» and «dis» can make opposites, as in «unkind» and «disagree».
fact: «Re» means again, «pre» means before, «sub» means under, and «super» means above.
fact: «Im», «il», «ir» and «in» often mean not, as in «impossible», «illegal», «irregular» and «incorrect».
fact: Maths units use prefixes too: kilo- means a thousand, centi- means a hundredth and milli- means a thousandth.
exercise: «Prefixes let you guess new words.» What does the prefix un- usually mean in «unhappy»? | *Not | Again | Before | Under
exercise: «Redo» is made from re- and do. What does re- mean here? | Before | *Again | Under | Above
exercise: A kilometre is made from kilo- and metre. How many metres are in one kilometre? | *1000 | 100 | 1000000 | 10
test: Which prefix makes «regular» mean not regular? | sub | un | pre | *ir
test: How many millimetres make one metre? | 1 | *1000 | 100 | 10
exam: Which prefix makes «kind» mean not kind? | sub | *un | pre | re
